 </div>
 </div>
</section>
<div class="distance-bar">📍 FOREST BOUNDARY ≈15 MI NORTH OF VERNAL VIA US-191 · FOREST HQ IS IN VERNAL</div>
<div class="content-wrap">

<div class="intro-pull"><strong>Vernal is not merely near Ashley National Forest — it is the forest's administrative home.</strong> The Ashley National Forest Supervisor's Office sits at 355 North Vernal Avenue, in town, and the Vernal Ranger District is one of its districts.<sup>1</sup> If you are planning a trip into the Uintas, the practical questions are which road to take, what is open in the season you are travelling, and where to sleep the night before an early start. This page answers those in order.</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>1. What Ashley National Forest Is</h2>
<p>Ashley National Forest covers about 1.4 million acres across northeastern Utah and southwestern Wyoming.<sup>1</sup> It is not a single block of trees: the forest spans three distinct landscapes — the northern and southern slopes of the Uinta Mountains, the Wyoming Basin, and the Tavaputs Plateau.<sup>2</sup></p>
<p>The elevation range is what makes trip planning here unusual. The forest runs from roughly 5,500 feet on the Green River to 13,528 feet at the summit of Kings Peak, the highest point in Utah.<sup>1</sup> A day that is warm and dry at the trailhead can be snowing above treeline.</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li>About 1.4 million acres, northeastern Utah and southwestern Wyoming<sup>1</sup></li>
 <li>276,175 acres of the High Uintas Wilderness lie within this forest<sup>1</sup></li>
 <li>Elevation 5,500 ft (Green River) to 13,528 ft (Kings Peak, Utah's highest)<sup>1</sup></li>
 <li>Flaming Gorge National Recreation Area is administered by Ashley National Forest<sup>1</sup></li>
 <li>Ranger districts include Vernal, Duchesne, Roosevelt, and Flaming Gorge (Manila, UT and Green River, WY)<sup>2</sup></li>
 <li>Forest Supervisor's Office: 355 North Vernal Avenue, Vernal, UT 84078 · (435) 789-1181<sup>1</sup></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>A note on the name.</strong> Several national forests share similar names and many share the phrase "national forest" in visitor searches. This page is about the <em>Ashley</em> National Forest in northeastern Utah and southwestern Wyoming — the one whose headquarters is in Vernal, Uintah County, and which contains Kings Peak and Flaming Gorge. It is not the Ashley River area of South Carolina, and it is unrelated to national forests of similar profile in other states.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>2. How Far It Is From Vernal</h2>
<p>From ${BUSINESS.address.full}, the nearest forest boundary is approximately 15 miles north via US-191. Driving times vary widely by destination because the forest is large and much of it is reached on unpaved roads — a boundary crossing is 20-odd minutes, while a High Uintas trailhead or the far side of the Red Cloud Loop is considerably longer.</p>
<p>The three routes below cover most visits. Which one you want depends entirely on what you came to do.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>3. Which Access Route Should You Use?</h2>
<h3>US-191 north — the main paved access</h3>
<p>US-191 leaving Vernal to the north is the primary paved route into the forest and on to Flaming Gorge. It forms part of the Flaming Gorge–Uintas National Scenic Byway, locally known as the Drive Through the Ages for the roadside geology markers along it.<sup>3</sup> The byway runs roughly 80 miles from Vernal north through the forest toward Manila and the Flaming Gorge area; as a round trip from Vernal with stops it is at least 150 miles and about five hours.<sup>3</sup> It is open year round, though ice and snow create hazards in winter.<sup>3</sup></p>
<p><strong>Use this if:</strong> you want paved road the whole way, you are heading for Flaming Gorge, or you have a passenger car.</p>

<h3>Red Cloud Loop — the unpaved scenic loop</h3>
<p>The Red Cloud Loop is roughly a 40-mile route through the forest north of Vernal. It can be picked up about 14 to 15 miles north of Vernal off US-191, or entered from the Dry Fork Canyon side near Maeser, just northwest of Vernal.<sup>4</sup> The majority of the route is unpaved, and portions close in winter.<sup>4</sup></p>
<p><strong>Use this if:</strong> you want canyon, aspen and meadow scenery and High Uintas views, and you have a vehicle suited to gravel roads. Check current conditions before committing — this is the route most affected by season and weather.</p>

<h3>District office first — the underrated option</h3>
<p>Because the Forest Supervisor's Office and the Vernal Ranger District are both in town,<sup>1</sup> you can get current road, fire and closure information in person before you drive, rather than discovering a gate on a forest road an hour out. For a first visit, or any trip in shoulder season, this is the single most useful stop in Vernal.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>4. Things To Do</h2>
<p>Almost every national forest supports hiking, fishing and camping, so the activity list alone tells you little. What distinguishes this one is what those activities are attached to: an 8,000-foot elevation spread<sup>1</sup> across three separate landscapes<sup>2</sup> inside a single boundary.</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>The High Uintas Wilderness</strong> — 276,175 acres of it lie inside this forest, including the approach to Kings Peak at 13,528 feet, the highest point in Utah.<sup>1</sup> This is the forest's headline feature, and the reason multi-day backcountry trips stage out of Vernal.</li>
 <li><strong>Flaming Gorge Reservoir</strong> — administered by Ashley National Forest rather than by a separate agency.<sup>1</sup> If the reservoir itself is your destination rather than the forest, our <a href="/hotel-near-flaming-gorge" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Flaming Gorge page</a> covers that trip specifically.</li>
 <li><strong>Two named drives</strong> — the Flaming Gorge–Uintas National Scenic Byway on pavement<sup>3</sup> and the Red Cloud Loop on gravel.<sup>4</sup> Which one suits your vehicle and season is covered in section 3 above.</li>
 <li><strong>Camping</strong> — developed Forest Service campgrounds and dispersed camping; sites and fees are listed on Recreation.gov.<sup>5</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Autumn hunting</strong> — a major draw in the Uintah Basin, and the season that fills Vernal lodging. Seasons, units and permits are set by Utah DWR, not the Forest Service (see section 6).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>For trail-level detail</strong> — individual hikes, specific campgrounds, fishing water and current trail conditions — <a href="https://adventuretoursvernal.com/guides/ultimate-guide-to-ashley-national-forest/" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Adventure Tours Vernal's recreation guide to this forest</a> goes deeper than a lodging page should, as does their <a href="https://adventuretoursvernal.com/camping/camping-in-ashley-national-forest/" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Ashley camping guide</a>. This page stays on the questions you answer before you leave town: which road, what season, and where to sleep the night before.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>5. Seasonal Planning</h2>
<p>Elevation, not the calendar, governs this forest. The 8,000-foot difference between the Green River and Kings Peak<sup>1</sup> means low-country and high-country seasons run on different schedules in the same week.</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Spring</strong> — lower elevations open first. High country and unpaved routes such as the Red Cloud Loop may still be snowbound or closed.<sup>4</sup> Verify road status before driving.</li>
 <li><strong>Summer</strong> — the full road network is generally available and High Uintas trailheads are reachable. Afternoon thunderstorms are a normal mountain risk at altitude.</li>
 <li><strong>Autumn</strong> — aspen colour along the byway and the Red Cloud Loop, and the busiest hunting period in the basin. Lodging in Vernal is at its tightest; book well ahead.</li>
 <li><strong>Winter</strong> — the US-191 byway stays open year round but ice and snow create hazards;<sup>3</sup> portions of the Red Cloud Loop close.<sup>4</sup></li>
</ul>
<div class="pro-tip"><p><strong>Before any shoulder-season trip:</strong> confirm road status with the Ashley National Forest office in Vernal, (435) 789-1181,<sup>1</sup> or on the forest website. Conditions on unpaved forest roads change faster than any web page — including this one.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>6. Permits and Regulations</h2>
<p>We deliberately do not restate regulations here. Getting a rule wrong has real consequences for a visitor, and both agencies below update their guidance more often than a hotel page can track. Use the primary sources:</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Forest use</strong> — camping, campfire and fire restrictions, motor-vehicle use maps, and any seasonal closures: <a href="https://www.fs.usda.gov/r04/ashley" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Ashley National Forest, U.S. Forest Service</a></li>
 <li><strong>Campground reservations and fees</strong> — <a href="https://www.recreation.gov" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Recreation.gov</a></li>
 <li><strong>Fishing and hunting licences, seasons, units and permits</strong> — <a href="https://wildlife.utah.gov/guidebooks" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Utah Division of Wildlife Resources guidebooks</a>. Season dates and permit numbers change year to year; always read the current guidebook.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>7. Planning Your Visit</h2>
<h3>How many days should you allow?</h3>
<p>A scenic-drive day on the byway needs about five hours of driving plus stops, as a round trip from Vernal.<sup>3</sup> The Red Cloud Loop is a comfortable half to full day given its length and surface.<sup>4</sup> Backcountry trips into the High Uintas are multi-day by nature. Two nights in Vernal covers a drive day plus one full day on foot or on the water; three or more suits hunting, fishing or backpacking.</p>
<h3>Can you combine it with Dinosaur National Monument?</h3>
<p>Yes, and most visitors staying in Vernal do. The two run in opposite directions from town — the forest is north, Dinosaur National Monument is east toward Jensen — so they work best as separate days from one base rather than as a single loop.</p>
<h3>What should you plan for?</h3>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Fuel and supplies</strong> — buy in Vernal. Services inside the forest are limited and seasonal.</li>
 <li><strong>Mobile coverage</strong> — treat the backcountry as having none. Download maps before leaving town and leave a plan with someone.</li>
 <li><strong>Weather at altitude</strong> — pack for conditions at your highest point, not at the trailhead. The elevation spread here is extreme.<sup>1</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Road surface</strong> — know whether your route is paved before you commit. Much of the forest road network is not.<sup>4</sup></li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>8. Staying in Vernal</h2>
<p>Vernal is the practical base for this forest: it is the largest town at its edge, it holds the forest's own headquarters,<sup>1</sup> and it is where you will find fuel, groceries, and outfitting before an early start.</p>
<p>Best Western Vernal Inn is at ${BUSINESS.address.full}. For forest trips specifically, what tends to matter is an early breakfast before a trailhead drive, somewhere to leave a truck and trailer, a kitchenette for packing food, and a weekly rate if you are here for a hunting or fishing season rather than a weekend.</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li>Free hot breakfast — before a trailhead start</li>
 <li>Free parking for trucks, trailers, ATV trailers and horse trailers</li>
 <li>Kitchenettes available — pack trail food and prep camp meals</li>
 <li>Pet friendly — ground-floor rooms available</li>
 <li>Weekly rates from ${usd(RATES.weekly.standardQueen)} (${perNight(RATES.weekly.standardQueen)}/night, ${RATES.taxNote}) — see <a href="/weekly-hotel-rates-vernal-utah" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">weekly rates</a></li>
 <li>24-hour front desk — check in around a hunting or shift schedule</li>
</ul>
</div>

